{
  "title": "Neuroblastoma",
  "url": "https://www.orthobullets.com/pathology/8081/neuroblastoma",
  "subtitle": "Updated: Jun 22 2021",
  "sections": [
    {
      "text": "summary",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Neuroblastomas are malignant tumors that develop from sympathetic neural tissue with most cases arising near the adrenal gland or the spinal cord.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "The condition usually presents in children younger than age 2 with fever, weight loss, and diarrhea.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "5",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Diagnosis is made with a biopsy showing small round blue cells forming rosette patterns.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "5",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Treatment may be observation or a combination of chemotherapy, stem cell transplantation, and surgical excision depending on the histological grade and location of the tumor.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Epidemiology",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Incidence",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "occurs in approximately 1 out of 100,000 children",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "it is the **most common solid tumor of childhood**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Demographics",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "5",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "slight male predominance",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "the majority of cases occur in children younger than 2 years of age",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Etiology",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Metastasis",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "metastasis to the bone are common and a **poor prognostic sign**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Presentation",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Symptoms",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "fever, malaise, weight loss, diarrhea",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "if the adrenal glands are affected, the child may present with significant tachycardia",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "abdominal mass",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Exam",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "may reveal abdominal mass or hepatosplenomegaly",
              "table": null,
              "images": [
                {
                  "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/8081/images/clinical%20photo%20neuroblastoma.jpg",
                  "caption": "clinical photograph showing a young child with a large abdominal neuroblastoma"
                }
              ],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Biopsy",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "needle or open incisional biopsy is required to obtain lesional tissue and make the histological definitive diagnosis",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Imaging",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Radiographs",
          "table": null,
          "images": [
            {
              "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/8081/images/neuroblastoma%20xray.jpg",
              "caption": "ap radiograph is the left proximal femur showing an ill defined lytic permeative bone lesion biopsy proven to be metastatic neuroblastoma"
            }
          ],
          "importance": "4",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "may show bone lesions in cases of metastatic disease",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "bone lesions are often permeative and lytic in nature",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "may occur anywhere in the skeleton",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "CT",
          "table": null,
          "images": [
            {
              "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/8081/images/neuroblastoma%20ct%20scan.jpg",
              "caption": "CT scan showing young child with a neuroblastoma of the lower abdomen"
            }
          ],
          "importance": "4",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "chest/abd/pelvis is the most common initial imaging sequence for evaluation of the extent of disease",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "helpful in determining the extent of visceral metastasis",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "MRI",
          "table": null,
          "images": [
            {
              "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/8081/images/neuroblastoma%20mri.jpg",
              "caption": "Child with neuroblastoma arising in the left upper quadrant as seen on MRI imaging"
            }
          ],
          "importance": "4",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "MRI scan of the chest/abdomen may be useful to delineate anatomic structures",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Bone scan",
          "table": null,
          "images": [
            {
              "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/8081/images/neuroblastoma%20bone%20scan.jpg",
              "caption": "young child with metastatic neuroblastoma and a bone lesion of the left femoral diaphysis"
            }
          ],
          "importance": "4",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "helpful in determining the extent of metastatic bone disease",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Histology",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "5",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Small round blue cells forming rosette patterns",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "other small round blue cell tumors of childhood include",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "rhabdomyosarcoma",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "non-Hodgkin's lymphoma",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "Ewing's sarcoma/PNET",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "blastemic component of Wilms’ tumor",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Treatment",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Nonoperative",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "observation",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "indications",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "very young infants with low risk tumors",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "2",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "a significant portion of neuroblastomas can spontaneously regress",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "2",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "chemotherapy and stem cell transplant alone",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "indications",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "children with high grade or metastatic neuroblastomas in a location not amenable to surgical resection",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "2",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "technique",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "treat with chemotherapeutics like platinum (cisplatin) or alkylating agents (ifosfamide) followed by stem cell transplant",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "2",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Operative",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "surgical excision",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "indications",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "low grade tumors which can be easily excised with a wide surgical margin while not damaging critical neurovascular structures",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "2",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "chemotherapy, stem cell transplant, and surgical excision",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "indications",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "high grade tumors which arise in close proximity to critical neurovascular structures and therefore cannot be easily excised with a wide surgical margin",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "2",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Prognosis",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "The **majority of children are cured**",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "4",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Metastatic disease is a poor prognostic factor",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "In very young children, **spontaneous regression** of the tumor without treatment is known to occur",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "4",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
